## The core decision: one retry owner

Attempts multiply, they don't add. If the SDK retries 3× and your wrapper retries 3×, a single logical call is 9 requests to the dependency — and your budget only ever sees 3 of them. So: **the wrapper owns retries, the SDK is pinned to one attempt.** If a given SDK can't be pinned, the alternative is to keep its retries and have the wrapper own only the budget + classification, with `MaxAttempts: 1`.

## `retry` package

```go
// Package retry bounds attempts against a single dependency. It owns capped
// exponential backoff with full jitter and a retry budget shared by every
// caller of that dependency, so extra attempts cannot sustain an outage.
//
// The wrapped client must be configured for exactly one attempt per call;
// see the package example. Retries at two layers multiply.
package retry

import (
	"context"
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"math"
	"math/rand/v2"
	"sync"
	"time"
)

// Retryable marks err as safe to replay: the failure is transient and the
// operation's contract permits another attempt. After, when positive, is a
// server-supplied lower bound on the wait (Retry-After, RateLimitReset).
type Retryable struct {
	Err   error
	After time.Duration
}

func (r *Retryable) Error() string { return r.Err.Error() }
func (r *Retryable) Unwrap() error { return r.Err }

// ErrBudgetExhausted reports that a replayable failure was not retried because
// the dependency's retry budget was spent.
var ErrBudgetExhausted = errors.New("retry budget exhausted")

// ErrNoTimeLeft reports that the caller's deadline cannot accommodate the
// required backoff, so no further attempt was started.
var ErrNoTimeLeft = errors.New("deadline too short to retry")
```

### Budget

Ratio-based token bucket: live traffic funds retries, so retries stay a fixed fraction of load no matter how many callers or goroutines are failing at once. A per-call `MaxAttempts` cannot do this — during a full outage every call independently spends its 3 attempts and triples fleet load.

```go
// Budget caps retries at a fraction of logical operations against one
// dependency. Each operation deposits one token (up to burst); each retry
// withdraws 1/ratio. A Budget is safe for concurrent use and must be shared
// by every caller of the dependency it protects.
type Budget struct {
	mu     sync.Mutex
	tokens float64
	max    float64
	cost   float64
}

// NewBudget returns a budget allowing roughly ratio retries per logical
// operation in steady state, with burst tokens of headroom for a cold or
// low-traffic client. Ratio is clamped to (0, 1]; burst to at least 1.
func NewBudget(ratio float64, burst int) *Budget {
	ratio = math.Min(math.Max(ratio, 0.001), 1)
	if burst < 1 {
		burst = 1
	}
	return &Budget{
		tokens: float64(burst),
		max:    float64(burst),
		cost:   1 / ratio,
	}
}

func (b *Budget) deposit() {
	b.mu.Lock()
	defer b.mu.Unlock()
	b.tokens = math.Min(b.tokens+1, b.max)
}

// withdraw reports whether a retry is affordable, spending a token if so.
func (b *Budget) withdraw() bool {
	b.mu.Lock()
	defer b.mu.Unlock()
	if b.tokens < b.cost {
		return false
	}
	b.tokens -= b.cost
	return true
}
```

### Policy

```go
// Policy is an immutable retry policy for one dependency.
type Policy struct {
	// MaxAttempts bounds total attempts including the first. Set it to 1 to
	// disable retries while keeping classification and metrics.
	MaxAttempts int

	// Base is the first backoff interval; Cap bounds every interval.
	Base time.Duration
	Cap  time.Duration

	// AttemptTimeout bounds a single attempt. Zero gives each attempt the
	// caller's whole remaining budget.
	AttemptTimeout time.Duration

	// Budget is required and must be shared across callers of the dependency.
	Budget *Budget

	// OnRetry, when set, observes each retry that is actually taken.
	OnRetry func(attempt int, delay time.Duration, err error)

	// OnSuppressed, when set, observes a replayable failure that was not
	// retried, with reason ErrBudgetExhausted, ErrNoTimeLeft, or nil for
	// MaxAttempts.
	OnSuppressed func(attempt int, err, reason error)

	// rand and sleep are test seams; the zero value uses the real ones.
	rand  func() float64
	sleep func(context.Context, time.Duration) error
}
```

`Do` is where the invariants live. Note the ordering: classify, then check attempts, then compute the delay, then check that the delay fits the caller's deadline, then charge the budget, then wait. Charging before waiting means a doomed call doesn't hold a slot for the whole backoff.

```go
// Do runs op until it succeeds, returns a non-replayable error, or the policy's
// attempt, time, or budget bounds are reached. Every attempt derives from ctx,
// so cancellation stops retries immediately and the caller's deadline covers
// queueing, backoff, attempts, and response handling alike.
//
// op must fully read and close any response before returning: its context is
// cancelled once it does.
func (p *Policy) Do(ctx context.Context, op func(ctx context.Context, attempt int) error) error {
	p.Budget.deposit()

	for attempt := 1; ; attempt++ {
		if err := ctx.Err(); err != nil {
			return err
		}

		err := p.attempt(ctx, attempt, op)
		if err == nil {
			return nil
		}

		var retryable *Retryable
		if !errors.As(err, &retryable) {
			return err
		}
		if attempt >= p.MaxAttempts {
			p.suppressed(attempt, err, nil)
			return fmt.Errorf("after %d attempts: %w", attempt, err)
		}

		delay := max(p.backoff(attempt), retryable.After)
		if !p.fits(ctx, delay) {
			p.suppressed(attempt, err, ErrNoTimeLeft)
			return fmt.Errorf("%w: %w", ErrNoTimeLeft, err)
		}
		if !p.Budget.withdraw() {
			p.suppressed(attempt, err, ErrBudgetExhausted)
			return fmt.Errorf("%w: %w", ErrBudgetExhausted, err)
		}
		if p.OnRetry != nil {
			p.OnRetry(attempt, delay, err)
		}
		if err := p.wait(ctx, delay); err != nil {
			return err
		}
	}
}

func (p *Policy) attempt(ctx context.Context, n int, op func(context.Context, int) error) error {
	if p.AttemptTimeout <= 0 {
		return op(ctx, n)
	}
	ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, p.AttemptTimeout)
	defer cancel()
	return op(ctx, n)
}

// backoff returns a full-jitter interval in [0, min(Cap, Base*2^(n-1))].
func (p *Policy) backoff(attempt int) time.Duration {
	window := float64(p.Base) * math.Pow(2, float64(attempt-1))
	if capped := float64(p.Cap); window > capped || math.IsInf(window, 1) {
		window = capped
	}
	return time.Duration(p.random() * window)
}

// fits reports whether delay plus a minimal attempt still fits the deadline.
// A server-supplied delay is a lower bound: it is never shortened to fit.
func (p *Policy) fits(ctx context.Context, delay time.Duration) bool {
	deadline, ok := ctx.Deadline()
	if !ok {
		return true
	}
	return time.Until(deadline) > delay+p.Base
}

func (p *Policy) wait(ctx context.Context, d time.Duration) error {
	if p.sleep != nil {
		return p.sleep(ctx, d)
	}
	t := time.NewTimer(d)
	defer t.Stop()
	select {
	case <-ctx.Done():
		return ctx.Err()
	case <-t.C:
		return nil
	}
}

func (p *Policy) random() float64 {
	if p.rand != nil {
		return p.rand()
	}
	return rand.Float64()
}

func (p *Policy) suppressed(attempt int, err, reason error) {
	if p.OnSuppressed != nil {
		p.OnSuppressed(attempt, err, reason)
	}
}
